 The webhook belongs to the bot's user (`webhooks.user_id`). Bots are users, so
 memberships, mentions, and authorship stay on the existing path.
 
+The same form has an "Add to all open rooms" switch. It is off by default, on
+both `/bots/new` and `/bots/:id/edit`. It is a real checkbox (styled as a
+switch).
+
+- Off: the bot joins no open room by itself. Someone can still add it to a
+  closed room, an open room, or a direct conversation the same way they add a
+  person.
+- On: the bot becomes a member of every open room that already exists, and of
+  every open room created afterwards (including a closed room that is later
+  opened). Direct conversations and closed rooms are unchanged.
+- Turning the switch off does not remove memberships the bot already has. It
+  only stops automatic joins from then on.
+- The open-room people list shows who actually has access: every person, bots
+  with the switch on, and anyone already a member. A bot left off the switch
+  is not listed there.
+
+### Joined callback
+
+Whenever a bot is added to a room — by the switch, because a new open room
+appeared while the switch was on, or because a person granted membership —
+its webhook receives one `joined` event for that room. Repeating a grant for
+a room the bot is already in does not send another event. Removing the bot
+and adding it again does, because that is a new membership.
+
+Direct conversations never send `joined`. A bot with no webhook is still added;
+there is just no HTTP call.
+
+The job is `Bot::DeliverJoinedWebhookJob`. It is enqueued after the membership
+insert commits, and it POSTs `Content-Type: application/json`:
+
+```json
+{
+  "event": "joined",
+  "room": { "id": 5, "name": "Ops", "reply_to": "/rooms/5/<bot-code>/messages" }
+}
+```
+
+- `room.reply_to` is the bot's posting URL for that room (relative), the same
+  path a mention uses.
+- There is no `author` and no `message`. The bot has not been spoken to yet.
+- A `200` `text/plain` or `text/html` body is posted into that room as the
+  bot's message, the same way a mention reply is. `204` and anything else are
+  ignored. That is how a bot says hello.
+
